# Class: obo:FOODON_03411227 (carrot plant)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:FOODON_03411227 obo:IAO_0000122)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:FOODON_03411227 "The carrot (*Daucus carota* subsp. *sativus*) is a root vegetable, usually orange, purple, red, white, or yellow in colour, with a crisp texture when fresh. The edible part of a carrot is a taproot. It is a domesticated form of the wild carrot *Daucus carota*, native to Europe and southwestern Asia. It has been bred for its greatly enlarged and more palatable, less woody-textured edible taproot, but is still the same species. [Wikipedia]
\\n\\n*Daucus carota* (common names include wild carrot, (UK) bird's nest, bishop's lace, and (US) Queen Anne's lace) is a flowering plant in the family *Apiaceae*, native to temperate regions of Europe, southwest Asia and naturalised to northeast North America; domesticated carrots are cultivars of a subspecies, *Daucus carota* subsp. *sativus*. Like the cultivated carrot, the wild carrot root is edible while young, but quickly becomes too woody to consume. [Wikipedia]"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym obo:FOODON_03411227 "daucus carota"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03411227 <http://eol.org/pages/581785>)
