# Class: obo:FOODON_03411737 (tumble mustard plant)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:FOODON_03411737 obo:IAO_0000428)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:FOODON_03411737 "Sisymbrium altissimum is a species of *Sisymbrium*. The plant is native to the western part of the Mediterranean Basin in Europe and Northern Africa and is widely naturalized throughout most of the world, including all of North America. 
Edible Parts: Leaves, Seed. Edible Uses: Condiment. Young leaves and shoots - raw or cooked. A somewhat hot flavour, they can be used as a flavouring in salads or cooked as a potherb. Seed - ground into a powder and used as a gruel or as a mustard-like flavouring in soups etc. [http://www.pfaf.org/user/Plant.aspx?LatinName=Sisymbrium+altissimum]"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03411737 <http://eol.org/pages/583815>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03411737 <http://www.ars-grin.gov/cgi-bin/npgs/html/taxon.pl?34451>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03411737 <http://www.itis.gov/servlet/SingleRpt/SingleRpt?search_topic=TSN&search_value=23312>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03411737 langual:B1737)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03411737 "PLANTS:SIAL2")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:FOODON_03411737 "tall rocket")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03411737 "sisymbrium altissimum"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:FOODON_03411737 "tumble mustard plant"@en)
SubClassOf(obo:FOODON_03411737 obo:FOODON_03411179)
SubClassOf(obo:FOODON_03411737 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002162 obo:NCBITaxon_71365))

# Class: obo:FOODON_03411738 (jicama plant)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:FOODON_03411738 obo:IAO_0000428)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:FOODON_03411738 "Pachyrhizus erosus, commonly known as Jícama, Yam, and Mexican Turnip, is the name of a native Mexican vine, although the name most commonly refers to the plant's edible tuberous root. Jícama is one species in the genus Pachyrhizus. Plants in this genus are commonly referred to as yam bean, although the term \"yam bean\" can be another name for jícama. The other major species of yam beans are also indigenous within the Americas."@en)
