# Class: obo:FOODON_03411819 (black crappie)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:FOODON_03411819 obo:IAO_0000428)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:FOODON_03411819 "The black crappie, *Pomoxis nigromaculatus* (Lesueur, 1829), is very similar to *P. annularis* in size, shape, and habits, except that it is darker, with a pattern of black spots. The black crappie has 7-8 spines on its dorsal fin. The number of spines on the dorsal fin, is occasionally the only way to differeniate between a juvenile black crappie and a white crappie. The black crappie tends to prefer clearer water than the white crappie does. Its native range is uncertain, since it has been so widely transplanted, but it is presumed to be similar to the white crappie's. The black crappie is also known as the strawberry bass or Oswego bass. ("@en)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03411819 <http://eol.org/pages/225242>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03411819 langual:B1819)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03411819 asfis:pxg)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03411819 "CEC1993:597")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03411819 "itis:168167")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03411819 "crappie, black"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03411819 "pomoxis nigromaculatus"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:FOODON_03411819 "black crappie"@en)
SubClassOf(obo:FOODON_03411819 obo:FOODON_03411409)
SubClassOf(obo:FOODON_03411819 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002162 obo:NCBITaxon_8182))

