# Class: obo:FOODON_03412307 (striped snakehead)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:FOODON_03412307 obo:IAO_0000428)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:FOODON_03412307 "Channa striata, the striped snakehead, is a species of snakehead fish. It is also known as the common snakehead, chevron snakehead and snakehead murrel. It is native to South and Southeast Asia, and has been introduced to some Pacific Islands. [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Channa_striata]"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412307 <http://eol.org/pages/205160>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412307 langual:B2307)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412307 asfis:fss)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412307 "itis:166667")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:FOODON_03412307 "banded snakehead")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:FOODON_03412307 "chevron snakehead")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:FOODON_03412307 "chevron snakehead"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:FOODON_03412307 "snakehead murrel")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03412307 "channa"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03412307 "channa striata"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03412307 "ophicephalus striatus"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03412307 "snakehead murrel"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:FOODON_03412307 "striped snakehead"@en)
SubClassOf(obo:FOODON_03412307 obo:FOODON_03412309)
SubClassOf(obo:FOODON_03412307 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002162 obo:NCBITaxon_64152))

# Class: obo:FOODON_03412308 (snakehead)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:FOODON_03412308 obo:IAO_0000428)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:FOODON_03412308 "The obscure snakehead, *Parachanna obscura*, is a medium-sized carnivorous fish that has an elongated shape tapered on both ends and is covered in medium circular scales (cycloid). The head, resembling a snake, is long and depressed anteriorly and covered with cycloid scales slightly larger than those scales on the body. This species is found in central Africa along the western coastline from as far north as Senegal to as far south as Zaire and into central Africa into southwest Sudan. [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obscure_snakehead]"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412308 <http://eol.org/pages/204348>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412308 langual:B2308)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412308 "itis:642606")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03412308 "ophicephalus obscurus"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03412308 "parachanna obscura"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03412308 "snake-head"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:FOODON_03412308 "snakehead"@en)
SubClassOf(obo:FOODON_03412308 obo:FOODON_03412309)
SubClassOf(obo:FOODON_03412308 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002162 obo:NCBITaxon_304449))

# Class: obo:FOODON_03412309 (snakehead family)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:FOODON_03412309 obo:IAO_0000428)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:FOODON_03412309 "The snakeheads are members of the freshwater perciform fish family *Channidae*, native to parts of Africa and Asia. These elongated, predatory fish are distinguished by their long dorsal fins, large mouths, and shiny teeth. They breathe air with gills, which allows them to migrate short distances over land. They are valuable as a food source and have become notorious as an intentionally released invasive species. [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snakehead_(fish)]"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412309 <http://eol.org/pages/5349>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412309 langual:B2309)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03412309 "channidae"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:FOODON_03412309 "snakehead family"@en)
SubClassOf(obo:FOODON_03412309 obo:FOODON_03411581)

# Class: obo:FOODON_03412310 (betel plant)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:FOODON_03412310 obo:IAO_0000428)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:FOODON_03412310 "The Betel (*Piper betle*) is a spice whose leaves have medicinal properties. The plant is evergreen and perennial, with glossy heart-shaped leaves and white catkins, and grows to a height of about 1 metre. The Betel plant originated in Malaysia and now grows in India, Indonesia and Sri Lanka. The best Betel leaf is the \"Magahi\" variety (literally from the Magadha region) grown near Patna in Bihar, India. The plant is known by a series of different names in the regions in which it is consumed - among these are Vetrilai (Tamil)"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:FOODON_03412310 "WIKIPEDIA:Betel).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412310 langual:B2310)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03412310 "betle pepper"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03412310 "piper betle"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:FOODON_03412310 "betel plant"@en)
SubClassOf(obo:FOODON_03412310 obo:FOODON_03413359)

# Class: obo:FOODON_03412311 (betel nut palm plant)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:FOODON_03412311 obo:IAO_0000428)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:FOODON_03412311 "Betel nut, also known as Pinang or Areca nut, is the seed of the Betel Palm (*Areca catechu*). Betel nuts are often chewed for their helpful effects, which are caused by the relatively high levels of alkaloids in the seed. Chewing betel nuts is an important and popular cultural activity in many Asian countries"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:FOODON_03412311 "WIKIPEDIA:Betel_nut")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412311 langual:B2311)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03412311 "areca catechu"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:FOODON_03412311 "betel nut palm plant"@en)
SubClassOf(obo:FOODON_03412311 obo:FOODON_03413359)

