# Class: obo:NCBITaxon_4538 (Oryza glaberrima)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:NCBITaxon_4538 wikipedia:Oryza_glaberrima)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym obo:NCBITaxon_4538 "Oryza glaberrima Steud.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym obo:NCBITaxon_4538 "Oryza glaberrima Steudel")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:NCBITaxon_4538 "red rice"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:NCBITaxon_4538 "It was first domesticated and grown in West Africa, and was brought to the Americas by enslaved West African rice farmers. It is now largely a subsistence crop, rarely sold in markets even in West Africa. While it has been partly replaced by higher-yielding Asian rice, and the number of varieties grown is declining, it persists, making up an estimated 20% of rice grown in West Africa.

In comparison to Asian rice, it is hardy, pest-resistant, low-labour, suited to a variety of African conditions, filling, and has a distinct nutty flavour. 
Crossbreeding between African and Asian rice is difficult, but there exist some crosses."@en)

